abharata", "context": "The cultural and historical context of ancient India frames the Mahabharata, providing insights into its social and ethical dimensions."}], "places": [{"name": "Kurukshetra, India", "role": "Battle site"}, {"name": "Hastinapura, India", "role": "Royal capital"}, {"name": "Varanasi, India", "role": "Cultural center"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Mahabharata", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "mahabharata"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q8276", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahabharata"], "tags": ["religious text", "Vedas, Upanishads & Hindu Texts", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Mahabharata is remembered as a landmark text of the Classical era, referenced in 167 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Mahabharata has profoundly influenced Indian culture and philosophy, shaping political thought and ethical discussions over centuries, evidenced by its adaptations and relevance in modern discourse.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-30T21:13:38Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28475993084", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Mahābhārata is a Hindu smriti epic poem from ancient India, one of the two important epics of Hinduism known as the Itihasas, the other being the Ramayana. It is also the longest poem ever written, consisting of over 100,000 verses. It narrates the events and aftermath of the Kurukshetra War, a war of succession between two groups of princely cousins, the Kauravas and the Pāṇḍavas. It contains philosophical and devotional material, such as a discussion of the four "goals of life" or puruṣārtha (12.161).", "newValue": "The Mahābhārata, an ancient Indian epic composed around the 4th century BCE to the 4th century CE, stands as a monumental text in Hindu literature and philosophy. Spanning the narrative of the Kurukshetra War between the Kauravas and Pāṇḍavas, it reflects the complexities of dharma (duty/righteousness) and has influenced spiritual and ethical thought across centuries. This epic, attributed to the sage Vyasa, not only narrates a grand tale but also encompasses diverse themes including governance, justice, and morality, making it a cornerstone of Indian culture and identity.

The epic unfolds through a myriad of sub-stories, including the Bhagavad Gita, which presents a dialogue between Prince Arjuna and the god Krishna on the battlefield, symbolizing the struggle between duty and ethics. Spanning over 100,000 verses, it serves both as a narrative and a philosophical treatise, discussing the four puruṣārthas (goals of life): dharma, artha (prosperity), kāma (pleasure), and moksha (liberation). The Mahābhārata's composition reflects a synthesis of various oral traditions and regional narratives, contributing significantly to the cultural and spiritual landscape of South Asia.

The legacy of the Mahābhārata extends beyond its narrative as it has been a source of inspiration for countless adaptations in literature, art, and performance across Asia. It has influenced political thought and social structures, particularly in the context of Hindu kingship and governance. The themes of the epic resonate in contemporary discussions on ethics and morality, showcasing its relevance in modern society. Moreover, various retellings and reinterpretations have allowed for its continued presence in global culture, establishing it as a pivotal work of literature."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-30T21:13:38Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28475993084", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Earlier literary tradition in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Text", "year": "3000 BCE"}, {"title": "Historical events in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Text", "year": "3000 BCE"}, {"title": "Patron commission in the 3000 BCE – 500 CE", "type": "Text", "year": "3000 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The oral traditions of ancient India laid the groundwork for the epic's narrative structure.", "The socio-political dynamics of the time fostered storytelling that addressed moral and ethical dilemmas.", "The consolidation of Hindu philosophy and mythology during the period influenced the themes and teachings embedded in the Mahābhārata."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-30T21:13:38Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28475993084", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Preserved knowledge through Mahabharata's historical significance", "type": "Text"}, {"title": "Influenced later works through Mahabharata's historical significance", "type": "Text"}, {"title": "Defined canon through Mahabharata's historical significance", "type": "Text"}], "newValue": ["The Mahābhārata has shaped Hindu cultural identity and philosophical discourse for over two millennia.", "Its narratives have influenced various art forms, including dance, theater, and literature, both in In